4. Let's understand the concept 
a) Hardware Layer

This is the bottom layer.

Hardware includes:

CPU
RAM
Hard Disk / SSD
Keyboard
Mouse
Network Card
GPU

Hardware performs the actual work.

Example

When you save a file:

echo "Linux" > notes.txt

The data is physically stored on the hard disk.

The disk is hardware.

b) Kernel

==> Real-Life Example

Imagine a traffic police officer.

Many vehicles want to use the road.

The police officer controls traffic.

Similarly,

Many applications want CPU and memory.

The kernel manages everything.

==> Functions of the Kernel
A) Process Management

Creates and manages processes.

Example:

firefox &

Kernel creates a process.

Check:

ps aux

B) Memory Management

Controls RAM usage.

Example:

free -h

Kernel decides:

Which process gets memory
How much memory is allocated

C) Device Management

Controls devices.

Examples:

Keyboard
Mouse
USB
Printer

Example:

lsblk

Shows storage devices managed by the kernel.

D) File System Management

Manages files and directories.

Example:

touch notes.txt

Kernel stores the file on disk.

E) Network Management

Handles network communication.

Example:

ping google.com

Kernel processes network packets.

c) Shell

The shell acts as an interpreter between the user and the kernel.

What is a Shell?

A shell accepts commands from users and passes them to the kernel.

Example

You type:

ls

The shell:

Reads the command.
Sends the request to the kernel.
Displays the result.

Output:

Documents
Downloads
Pictures

Check Current Shell
echo $SHELL

Example
/bin/bash

